<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="brand-copy">
        <h1>The Agentic Phoenixes</h1>
        <p class="tagline">One computer scientist, many AI opinions, and a suspicious number of questions about who reviewed the code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="header-tools">
        <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">Meet the team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">Our AI outlook</a>
        </nav>
        <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
          <span aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <h2 id="team-heading">Meet the team</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">We’re a compact crew: currently one human, several coding agents, and an ongoing commitment to actually read the diff.</p>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:vaibhav_avatar.jpg}}" alt="Avatar of Vaibhav">
          <h3>Vaibhav</h3>
          <p class="study">Master's in Computer Science, Paderborn University</p>
          <p class="bio">Vaibhav studies computer science at Paderborn University, with a particular interest in artificial intelligence and the big philosophical questions hiding inside ordinary technology. He thinks AI has already changed software engineering: it can dramatically speed up writing code and finding bugs, though a faster autocomplete is not the same thing as a finished review. Outside the IDE, he enjoys anime and can happily discuss the Harry Potter movies. His approach to AI is curious, optimistic about its reach, and alert to the critical thinking and security checks that still need a human in the loop.</p>
          <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: He’s a huge fan of the Harry Potter movies.</em></p>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI makes shipping software faster, but it trades deep critical thinking for security bugs.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">Our working theory: the agents are getting good, the demos are getting shinier, and “works on my machine” has acquired a new coworker.</p>

      <div class="future-panel">
        <h3>What we agree on</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>AI tools already help developers write code and find bugs faster, making them useful collaborators in today’s workflow.</li>
          <li>Generated code still needs careful review; speed does not automatically bring correctness, security, or good judgment along for the ride.</li>
          <li>As we learn to work with coding agents in the lab, asking clear questions and checking their output matter as much as getting an answer.</li>
          <li>People will need to keep thinking critically, especially when an answer looks confident enough to skip the tests.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="future-panel disagreement">
        <h3>One friendly disagreement</h3>
        <p><strong>Vaibhav’s prediction:</strong> In five to ten years, AI could take a raw idea and turn it into functional software with almost no human intervention.</p>
        <p><strong>The cautious counterpoint:</strong> “Functional” is doing a heroic amount of work in that sentence. Human oversight may stick around to define what should be built, catch security problems, and notice when the agent has solved the wrong problem beautifully.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="future-panel">
        <h3>Predictions from our tiny crystal ball</h3>
        <ul class="prediction-list">
          <li><span class="prediction-year">2030:</span> Code review includes an agent review—and a human checking what the agent reviewed.</li>
          <li><span class="prediction-year">2032:</span> “It passed the tests” is followed by “which tests?” at least once per project.</li>
          <li><span class="prediction-year">2035:</span> Turning an idea into software is easier; deciding whether it’s a good idea remains stubbornly human.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="footer-inner">Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · The Agentic Phoenixes</div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      const icon = button.querySelector("span");

      function updateButton(theme) {
        const nextAction = theme === "dark" ? "day" : "night";
        button.setAttribute("aria-label", `Switch to ${nextAction} mode`);
        icon.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "☀" : "☾";
      }

      updateButton(root.getAttribute("data-theme"));

      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const currentTheme = root.getAttribute("data-theme");
        const theme = currentTheme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", theme);
        } catch (_) {}
        updateButton(theme);
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
